Thanks for all the replies.

The following suggestions were made:

1) Use psrinfo - This just tells me that the CPUs are up and running.  Not
   usage reports on each processor.

2) Use /usr/bin/X11/demos/cpuinfo - This provided some assurance that both
   CPUs are being used.

3) Use Monitor: gatekeeper.dec.com:/pub/DEC/monitor.alpha.tar.Z - I didn't
   try this but I'm assuming that it is similar to cpuinfo.

4) Use Performance Manager from Associated Products CD - I haven't tried 
   this yet...but it sounds cool.

5) Use vmubc ftp://ftp.digital.com/pub/Digital/ - Haven't tried it but it 
   sounds good.

6) Use collect ftp://ftp.digital.com/pub/Digital/collect/ - Haven't tried it
   but it sounds good.

7) Use kdbx:

	kdbx -k /vmunix
	(kdbx)cpustat -update 4

   This worked out great and completely convinced both CPUs were active.
   Thought it didn't tell me what PID was running on which CPU.  Nor does
   it give any information on threads (Though, I haven't found a utility 
   that does).

8) Run the sample code that comes with PSE - I'm going to try this.


