=0A=
	GNU CC writes a `.verstamp' directive to the assembler output file=0A=
	unless it is built as a cross-compiler.  It gets the version to=0A=
	use from the system header file `/usr/include/stamp.h'.  If you=0A=
	install a new version of DEC Unix, you should rebuild GCC to pick=0A=
	up the new version stamp.=0A=
=0A=
	Note that since the Alpha is a 64-bit architecture,=0A=
	cross-compilers from 32-bit machines will not generate code as=0A=
	efficient as that generated when the compiler is running on a=0A=
	64-bit machine because many optimizations that depend on being=0A=
	able to represent a word on the target in an integral value on the=0A=
	host cannot be performed.  Building cross-compilers on the Alpha=0A=
	for 32-bit machines has only been tested in a few cases and may=0A=
	not work properly.=0A=
=0A=
	`make compare' may fail on old versions of DEC Unix unless you add=0A=
	`-save-temps' to `CFLAGS'.  On these systems, the name of the=0A=
	assembler input file is stored in the object file, and that makes=0A=
	comparison fail if it differs between the `stage1' and `stage2'=0A=
	compilations.  The option `-save-temps' forces a fixed name to be=0A=
	used for the assembler input file, instead of a randomly chosen=0A=
	name in `/tmp'.  Do not add `-save-temps' unless the comparisons=0A=
	fail without that option.  If you add `-save-temps', you will have=0A=
	to manually delete the `.i' and `.s' files after each series of=0A=
	compilations.=0A=
=0A=
	GNU CC now supports both the native (ECOFF) debugging format used=0A=
	by DBX and GDB and an encapsulated STABS format for use only with=0A=
	GDB.  See the discussion of the `--with-stabs' option of=0A=
	`configure' below for more information on these formats and how to=0A=
	select them.=0A=
=0A=
	There is a bug in DEC's assembler that produces incorrect line=0A=
	numbers for ECOFF format when the `.align' directive is used.  To=0A=
	work around this problem, GNU CC will not emit such alignment=0A=
	directives while writing ECOFF format debugging information even=0A=
	if optimization is being performed.  Unfortunately, this has the=0A=
	very undesirable side-effect that code addresses when `-O' is=0A=
	specified are different depending on whether or not `-g' is also=0A=
	specified.=0A=
=0A=
	To avoid this behavior, specify `-gstabs+' and use GDB instead of=0A=
	DBX.  DEC is now aware of this problem with the assembler and=0A=
